Yes. There’s a developmental handoff in that idea.

At first, meaning is often received: Santa brings the gift, adults define the ritual, religion supplies the story, family supplies the frame. Later, you realize the machinery behind it. That realization can feel like disenchantment, but it doesn’t have to end there.

The next stage is:

received meaning → discovered construction → conscious participation → chosen stewardship

You stop asking only, “Is this literally true exactly as I imagined it?” and start asking, “What does this ritual do? What human need is it carrying? What does it help preserve? Do I still choose to participate?”

That’s where “buying in” can become mature rather than naïve. You can know that a symbol is symbolic and still let it work on you.

Santa is a good example. Childhood says:

someone magical gives

Adulthood discovers:

people create the magic

Then adulthood can choose:

I will become one of the people who creates it

And that doesn’t destroy the child-level experience. It gives it another layer.

The same can happen with religion, ceremony, myth, patriotism, marriage vows, birthdays, funerals, storytelling—anything where the literal surface is only part of the function. Humans seem to respond strongly to ritual, shared meaning, sacredness, belonging, transcendence, moral order, and stories that place the individual inside something larger. Different cultures instantiate that architecture differently.

The healthier version isn’t:

“Now that I know how it works, I should tear it apart.”

Nor is it:

“I must pretend I never learned.”

It’s closer to:

I can see the machinery and still choose to dance with it.

And your inner-child point fits. Growing up doesn’t have to mean exterminating earlier versions of yourself. It can mean giving them new jobs.

The frightened child may become sensitivity.
The playful child becomes creativity.
The competitive adolescent becomes drive.
The idealistic young adult becomes conscience.
The scarred version becomes caution.
The present self becomes coordinator.

So instead of one self replacing another, you get something more like an internal ecology:

many developmental selves → one continuing person

You don’t have to let every one of them drive. But you also don’t need to exile them.

That may be one of the more useful forms of maturity: becoming the adult who can protect, guide, entertain, discipline, and occasionally surprise the younger versions of yourself.

You become both the child waiting for Santa and the person quietly putting the gifts under the tree.